examples.text:
Connect Froala Editor to a small Node.js server and everyone in a document sees edits as they happen. Comments, suggestions and saved versions go to SQLite, so they stay in place after a reload. Everything runs on your own server, with no third-party collaboration cloud.

### How real-time collaboration works in Froala

To add real-time collaboration to Froala Editor, turn on the collaboration plugin with `collabConfig` and connect it to a small Node.js server. The server has two jobs. A WebSocket relay passes live edits between everyone in the same document. REST routes from the [Froala Node.js SDK](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/sdks/nodejs/collaboration-overview/) store comments, suggestions, versions and the latest HTML in SQLite.

The demo is running on [CodeSandbox](https://codesandbox.io/p/devbox/modern-breeze-9z4tv8?file=%2Findex.js).

#### Things to try

- Select some text and press `Ctrl + M` (`Cmd + M` on Mac) to add a comment.
- Switch the mode selector to Suggesting, then delete a word. The deletion becomes a suggestion (like track changes in Word) that another person can accept or reject.
- Save a named version from the version history menu, change a few words, then restore it.
- Reload the page. Your comments and versions are still there because they live in the database.

### Example code with Node.js, WebSockets and SQLite

The example has four files. The editor page and the API share one origin, which keeps cookies and CORS simple.

#### public/index.html

The editor page. collabConfig points the collaboration plugin at the server, and a short fetch seeds new sessions with the last saved copy.

```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Froala collaboration example</title>
    <link
      rel="stylesheet"
      href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@5.6.0/css/froala_editor.pkgd.min.css"
    />
    <link
      rel="stylesheet"
      href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@5.6.0/css/froala_style.min.css"
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <div id="editor"></div>

    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/froala-editor@5.6.0/js/froala_editor.pkgd.min.js"></script>
    <script>
      // The API and the WebSocket relay share this page's origin.
      const origin = window.location.origin;
      const wsOrigin = origin.replace(/^http/, "ws");

      // TEMPORARY: one shared room. In your app, read the id from the route.
      const docId = "my-doc";

      // TEMPORARY: starter text for a brand-new room. In your app, use "".
      const STARTER_HTML =
        "<h2>Welcome to the collaborative editor</h2>" +
        "<p>Everyone who opens this page edits this document at the same time.</p>";

      // The relay keeps no history, so seed the editor with the last copy
      // the save plugin stored. Keep this fetch in your app.
      async function loadSavedHtml() {
        try {
          const res = await fetch(origin + "/collab/" + docId + "/content");
          if (!res.ok) return STARTER_HTML;
          const data = await res.json();
          return data.content || STARTER_HTML;
        } catch (err) {
          return STARTER_HTML;
        }
      }

      function startEditor(html) {
        document.getElementById("editor").innerHTML = html;

        new FroalaEditor("#editor", {
          // TEMPORARY: inject your key from the server when you render the page.
          key: "YOUR-LICENSE-KEY",
          heightMin: 300,
          heightMax: 700,
          htmlExecuteScripts: false,

          collabConfig: {
            docId,
            // TEMPORARY: a random guest. In your app, pass the signed-in user.
            user: {
              id: "u-" + Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 7),
              name: "Guest",
            },
            commentsUrl: origin + "/collab/" + docId + "/comments",
            suggestionsUrl: origin + "/collab/" + docId + "/suggestions",
            versionControl: { url: origin + "/collab/" + docId + "/versions" },
            realTime: { syncUrl: wsOrigin },
          },

          // Keeps a server copy of the latest HTML through the save plugin.
          saveURL: origin + "/collab/" + docId + "/content",
          saveParam: "body",
          saveInterval: 5000,

          events: {
            "collab.connectionStatus": function (status) {
              console.log("connection:", status);
            },
            "collab.synced": function () {
              // Load stored comments and suggestions into the live session.
              this.collaborative.syncCollabData();
            },
          },
        });
      }

      loadSavedHtml().then(startEditor);
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

#### index.js

Relays live edits over WebSocket and stores comments, suggestions and versions through the Froala Node.js SDK. Security checks run in front of every route.

```
/**
 * Froala collaboration backend, hardened.
 *
 * Everything you must fill in yourself is marked TODO and lives in the
 * "YOUR APPLICATION" block near the top. Nothing else should need editing.
 *
 * Install:
 *   npm install express cors express-rate-limit sanitize-html dotenv \
 *     ws better-sqlite3 wysiwyg-editor-node-sdk
 *
 * ws and better-sqlite3 arrive with the SDK, but declare them explicitly
 * since this file requires them directly.
 */
require("dotenv").config(); // first line of index.js

const http = require("http");
const path = require("path");
const fs = require("fs");
const express = require("express");
const cors = require("cors");
const rateLimit = require("express-rate-limit");
const sanitizeHtml = require("sanitize-html");
const Database = require("better-sqlite3");
const { WebSocketServer } = require("ws");
const {
  Collaborative,
  CollabPersistence,
  VersionControl,
  AsyncSave,
} = require("wysiwyg-editor-node-sdk");

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * Configuration. Everything comes from the environment.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

const PORT = Number(process.env.PORT || 3000);

// Origins allowed to call this server and to open WebSockets against it.
// Comma separated, e.g. "https://app.example.com,https://admin.example.com"
const ALLOWED_ORIGINS = (process.env.ALLOWED_ORIGINS || "")
  .split(",")
  .map((s) => s.trim())
  .filter(Boolean);

const DB_PATH =
  process.env.DB_PATH || path.join(__dirname, "data", "collab.db");

// Autosaved versions older than this are pruned. Named versions are kept.
const RETENTION_DAYS = Number(process.env.RETENTION_DAYS || 30);

if (ALLOWED_ORIGINS.length === 0) {
  console.error("ALLOWED_ORIGINS is empty. Refusing to start.");
  process.exit(1);
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * YOUR APPLICATION
 *
 * Three functions stand between the internet and your data. Replace all
 * three. They fail closed as written, so the server is safe but useless
 * until you do.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

function parseCookies(header) {
  const out = {};
  for (const part of (header || "").split(";")) {
    const i = part.indexOf("=");
    if (i < 0) continue;
    const k = part.slice(0, i).trim();
    if (k) out[k] = decodeURIComponent(part.slice(i + 1).trim());
  }
  return out;
}

/**
 * Resolve the signed-in user from the request's cookies.
 * Return null for anyone who is not signed in.
 *
 * @returns {Promise<{ userId: string, name: string } | null>}
 */
async function getSession(cookieHeader) {
  // TEMPORARY dev bypass. Delete this block and DEV_OPEN_ACCESS in .env.
  if (process.env.DEV_OPEN_ACCESS === "true") {
    return { userId: "dev-user", name: "Dev User" };
  }

  const cookies = parseCookies(cookieHeader);
  const sid = cookies.sid;
  if (!sid) return null;

  // TODO: look sid up in your session store.
  // TEMPORARY: remove the next line. Until you do, anyone with a sid
  // cookie is treated as signed in.
  return { userId: "dev-user", name: "Dev User" };
  return null;
}

/**
 * May this user open this document at all?
 *
 * @returns {Promise<boolean>}
 */
async function canOpenDoc(userId, docId) {
  // TODO: check your documents/permissions tables.
  // return await db.documentIsVisibleTo(docId, userId);
  return true; // TEMPORARY: remove this line. Lets the demo open any document.
  return false;
}

/**
 * What may this user do in this document?
 * "editor" writes, "suggester" proposes changes, "viewer" reads only.
 *
 * This is the authoritative answer. collabConfig.user.role in the browser
 * only decides which buttons get drawn.
 *
 * @returns {Promise<"editor" | "suggester" | "viewer">}
 */
async function roleFor(userId, docId) {
  // TODO: derive from your own permissions model.
  return "editor"; // TEMPORARY: remove this line. Makes everyone an editor.
  return "viewer";
}

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * Database
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

fs.mkdirSync(path.dirname(DB_PATH), { recursive: true });

// Write-ahead logging so readers do not block writers. Stored in the file,
// so this only has to happen once, but running it every boot is harmless.
new Database(DB_PATH).pragma("journal_mode = WAL");

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * Express. Order matters: everything below runs before attachRoutes.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

const app = express();

// CodeSandbox runs behind Cloudflare, and production runs behind a proxy
// too. Without this, req.ip is the proxy's address, and express-rate-limit
// raises a validation error when it sees X-Forwarded-For.
app.set("trust proxy", 1);
app.disable("x-powered-by");

// 1. CORS. First, so that rejections further down still carry CORS headers
// and show up in the browser as a real status rather than a CORS error.
app.use(
  cors({
    origin: (origin, cb) => {
      // Same-origin and server-to-server requests send no Origin header.
      if (!origin) return cb(null, true);
      cb(null, ALLOWED_ORIGINS.includes(origin));
    },
    credentials: true,
  })
);

// 2. Body parsing. Version snapshots are whole HTML documents, so the
// Express default of 100kb rejects them with a 413.
app.use(express.json({ limit: "5mb" }));
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: true, limit: "5mb" }));

// Serve the editor page. Public, no auth, same origin as the API.
app.use(express.static("public"));

// 3. No caching of collaboration data. A browser that serves a stale
// comment list from cache shows people a document that has moved on.
app.use("/collab/:docId", (req, res, next) => {
  res.set("Cache-Control", "no-store");
  next();
});

// 4. Never leak database internals. The SDK's 500 responses carry the raw
// SQLite message, which names your tables and columns.
app.use("/collab/:docId", (req, res, next) => {
  const send = res.json.bind(res);
  res.json = (body) => {
    if (res.statusCode >= 500) {
      console.error("collab %s %s", req.method, req.originalUrl, body);
      return send({ error: "server error" });
    }
    return send(body);
  };
  next();
});

// 5. docId validation. It flows into URL paths and database keys, so reject
// anything that is not plainly a document name before it travels further.
const DOC_ID = /^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_-]{0,63}$/;

app.use("/collab/:docId", (req, res, next) => {
  if (!DOC_ID.test(req.params.docId)) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: "invalid docId" });
  }
  next();
});

// 6. Authentication and authorization. The only thing that actually
// protects the data.
app.use("/collab/:docId", async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const session = await getSession(req.headers.cookie);
    if (!session) {
      return res.status(401).json({ error: "not signed in" });
    }

    const { docId } = req.params;
    if (!(await canOpenDoc(session.userId, docId))) {
      return res.status(403).json({ error: "no access to this document" });
    }

    const role = await roleFor(session.userId, docId);

    // Viewers may read and nothing else.
    if (req.method !== "GET" && role === "viewer") {
      return res.status(403).json({ error: "read only" });
    }

    req.user = { id: session.userId, name: session.name, role };
    next();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("auth failed", err);
    res.status(500).json({ error: "server error" });
  }
});

// 7. Rate limiting. Reads are cheap. Writes are what a client bug turns
// into a loop against your database.
const writeLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 60_000,
  limit: 120,
  standardHeaders: true,
  legacyHeaders: false,
  // Budget per person per document, not per IP, so one office behind one
  // address does not throttle itself.
  keyGenerator: (req) => `${req.user.id}:${req.params.docId}`,
  message: { error: "too many requests" },
});

app.use("/collab/:docId", (req, res, next) =>
  req.method === "GET" ? next() : writeLimiter(req, res, next)
);

// 8. Sanitization on the way in. Content is returned verbatim by the SDK,
// so this is the only gate before it reaches storage.
const POLICY = {
  // Match this to what your toolbar can actually produce. Stricter than the
  // editor is fine. Looser is a hole.
  allowedTags: [
    "p", "br", "strong", "em", "u", "s", "sub", "sup",
    "h1", "h2", "h3", "h4", "blockquote", "pre", "code",
    "ul", "ol", "li", "a", "img", "hr", "span", "div",
    "table", "thead", "tbody", "tr", "th", "td",
  ],
  allowedAttributes: {
    a: ["href", "title", "target", "rel"],
    img: ["src", "alt", "width", "height"],
    span: ["style"],
    div: ["style"],
    td: ["colspan", "rowspan"],
    th: ["colspan", "rowspan"],
  },
  allowedStyles: {
    "*": {
      "text-align": [/^left$|^right$|^center$|^justify$/],
      color: [/^#[0-9a-fA-F]{3,8}$/, /^rgba?\(/],
      "background-color": [/^#[0-9a-fA-F]{3,8}$/, /^rgba?\(/],
    },
  },
  // Blocks javascript: and data: URLs.
  allowedSchemes: ["http", "https", "mailto"],
  transformTags: {
    a: sanitizeHtml.simpleTransform("a", { rel: "noopener noreferrer" }),
  },
};

// Comment bodies, replies and suggestion text are plain text, not rich
// content. Strip every tag rather than allowing a subset.
const TEXT_ONLY = { allowedTags: [], allowedAttributes: {} };

app.use("/collab/:docId", (req, res, next) => {
  const body = req.body;
  if (!body || typeof body !== "object") return next();

  const text = (value) =>
    typeof value === "string" ? sanitizeHtml(value, TEXT_ONLY) : value;

  // Document content. AsyncSave posts "body", VersionControl posts "snapshot".
  if (typeof body.body === "string") {
    body.body = sanitizeHtml(body.body, POLICY);
  }
  if (typeof body.snapshot === "string") {
    body.snapshot = sanitizeHtml(body.snapshot, POLICY);
  }

  // Plain-text fields: version title and description, comment text,
  // suggestion before and after text, and author display names.
  // Only keys already in the request are touched. The SDK's PATCH handlers
  // treat any key that exists, even an undefined one, as a requested change.
  for (const key of [
    "title",
    "description",
    "text",
    "originalText",
    "suggestedText",
    "authorName",
  ]) {
    if (key in body) body[key] = text(body[key]);
  }

  // Version author.
  if (body.author && typeof body.author === "object") {
    body.author.name = text(body.author.name);
  }

  // Replies, on both comments and suggestions. PATCH replaces the whole
  // array, so every element passes through here on every update.
  if (Array.isArray(body.replies)) {
    body.replies = body.replies.map((reply) => {
      if (!reply || typeof reply !== "object") return reply;
      return {
        ...reply,
        text: text(reply.text),
        authorName: text(reply.authorName),
      };
    });
  }

  next();
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * The SDK's own routes. Everything above is already in front of them.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

CollabPersistence.attachRoutes(app, { dbPath: DB_PATH });
VersionControl.attachRoutes(app, { dbPath: DB_PATH });
AsyncSave.attachRoutes(app, { dbPath: DB_PATH });

// Liveness only. No document data, safe to leave open for your load
// balancer. Move it behind auth if you would rather not publish counts.
app.get("/health", (req, res) => res.json(Collaborative.getStats()));

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * WebSocket relay, with the same checks on the upgrade.
 *
 * Note: we manage the ws server ourselves rather than calling
 * Collaborative.attachToServer(), because that attaches before there is
 * any opportunity to reject a connection.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

const server = http.createServer(app);
const wss = new WebSocketServer({ noServer: true });

server.on("upgrade", async (req, socket, head) => {
  const reject = (why) => {
    console.warn(
      "upgrade rejected:", why,
      "| url:", req.url,
      "| origin:", req.headers.origin
    );
    socket.destroy();
  };

  try {
    if (!ALLOWED_ORIGINS.includes(req.headers.origin)) {
      return reject("origin not allowed");
    }

    const session = await getSession(req.headers.cookie);
    if (!session) return reject("no session");

    const docId = decodeURIComponent(req.url.split("?")[0].slice(1));
    if (!DOC_ID.test(docId)) return reject(`bad docId: "${docId}"`);

    if (!(await canOpenDoc(session.userId, docId))) {
      return reject("canOpenDoc said no");
    }

    console.log("upgrade accepted:", docId);
    wss.handleUpgrade(req, socket, head, (conn) => {
      Collaborative.setupWSConnection(conn, req);
    });
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("upgrade error", err);
    socket.destroy();
  }
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * Retention. Autosaves are a full copy of the document every interval,
 * so they are where the volume comes from. Named versions are kept.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

function pruneAutosaves() {
  try {
    const db = new Database(DB_PATH);
    const cutoff = Date.now() - RETENTION_DAYS * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;
    const result = db
      .prepare(
        `DELETE FROM versions
         WHERE is_named = 0 AND source = 'auto' AND created_at < ?`
      )
      .run(cutoff);
    db.close();
    if (result.changes) {
      console.log("pruned %d autosaved versions", result.changes);
    }
  } catch (err) {
    // Non-fatal. Most likely the table does not exist yet on first boot.
    console.error("prune failed", err.message);
  }
}

setInterval(pruneAutosaves, 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000).unref();

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ *
 * Start and stop
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------ */

server.listen(PORT, "0.0.0.0", () => {
  console.log("Collaboration backend listening on %d", PORT);
  console.log("Allowed origins: %s", ALLOWED_ORIGINS.join(", "));
});

function shutdown() {
  console.log("shutting down");
  // 1001 is "going away". Clients reconnect after reconnectDelay.
  for (const client of wss.clients) {
    client.close(1001, "server restarting");
  }
  server.close(() => process.exit(0));
  // Do not hang forever on a stuck connection.
  setTimeout(() => process.exit(1), 10_000).unref();
}

process.on("SIGTERM", shutdown);
process.on("SIGINT", shutdown);
```

#### package.json

Dependencies for the server. Requires Node.js 22.12 or newer.

```
{
  "name": "froala-collaboration-server",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "private": true,
  "main": "index.js",
  "scripts": {
    "start": "node index.js"
  },
  "dependencies": {
    "better-sqlite3": "^12.10.0",
    "cors": "^2.8.6",
    "dotenv": "^18.0.1",
    "express": "^5.2.1",
    "express-rate-limit": "^8.7.0",
    "sanitize-html": "^2.17.7",
    "ws": "^8.21.3",
    "wysiwyg-editor-node-sdk": "^5.5.0"
  },
  "overrides": {
    "htmlparser2": "^10.1.0"
  }
}
```

#### .env

Server settings. The server exits at startup until ALLOWED_ORIGINS has a value.

```
# The origin your editor page is served from, with no trailing slash.
# In a CodeSandbox fork, use your fork's preview URL, e.g. https://abc123-3000.csb.app
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000
DB_PATH=./data/collab.db
RETENTION_DAYS=30

# TEMPORARY: open access for the demo. Delete before launch.
DEV_OPEN_ACCESS=true
```

#### Run it locally

1. Install Node.js 22.12 or newer. The `htmlparser2` override in `package.json` keeps `sanitize-html` loading on earlier Node.js 20 releases.
2. Put `index.js`, `package.json` and `.env` in one folder, and the editor page in `public/index.html`.
3. Replace `YOUR-LICENSE-KEY` with the trial key from the [Froala download page](https://froala.com/wysiwyg_editor-download/). The trial turns on every plugin, collaboration included.
4. Run the commands below, then open `http://localhost:3000` in two browser windows.

```
npm install
npm start

# In a second terminal
curl http://localhost:3000/health
# {"docs":0,"clients":0}
```

With both windows open, `/health` reports one document and two clients. Reload a window and check that your comments are still listed. That confirms the database side is connected, since the relay holds live edits in memory only.

#### Server checks

Every request to `/collab/:docId` passes through these layers in order before it reaches the SDK routes.

#, LAYER, WHAT IT DOES
1, CORS, Accepts browser requests from `ALLOWED_ORIGINS` only. It runs first so later rejections still reach the browser as a readable status.
2, Body size, Raises the JSON limit to 5 MB, since version snapshots are whole HTML documents.
3, Caching, Sends `Cache-Control: no-store` so browsers always fetch the current comment list.
4, Error masking, Replaces database details in 500 responses with a generic message and logs the full error on the server.
5, docId check, Accepts letters, numbers, dashes and underscores, up to 64 characters.
6, Sign-in and roles, Calls `getSession`, `canOpenDoc` and `roleFor`. Viewers get read-only access.
7, Rate limit, Allows 120 writes per minute for each person in each document. Reads are unlimited.
8, Sanitization, Cleans document HTML against the `POLICY` allow list and strips every tag from comments, suggestions and names.

WebSocket connections get the same origin, session, docId and canOpenDoc checks before the server places them in a room. A daily job deletes autosaved versions older than RETENTION_DAYS and keeps every named version.

#### Endpoints

METHOD, PATH, PURPOSE
GET, POST, `/collab/:docId/comments`, List and create comments.
PATCH, DELETE, `/collab/:docId/comments/:id`, Resolve, reply to or delete a comment.
GET, POST, `/collab/:docId/suggestions`, List and create suggestions.
PATCH, DELETE, `/collab/:docId/suggestions/:id`, Accept, reject, reply to or delete a suggestion.
GET, POST, `/collab/:docId/versions`, List and save versions.
GET, PATCH, DELETE, `/collab/:docId/versions/:id`, Read, rename or delete one version.
GET, POST, `/collab/:docId/content`, Read and save the latest document HTML.
WS, `/:docId`, The live relay. Same checks as above, applied to the upgrade request.
GET, `/health`, Open room and client counts for your load balancer.

### Prepare your collaboration server for production

The demo uses open access so anyone can try it. These steps turn it into a server you can put in front of real users.

#### Connect your sign-in system

Replace the three functions in the `YOUR APPLICATION` block of `index.js`. Everything else in the file is configuration.

```
// Read your session cookie and return the signed-in user, or null.
async function getSession(cookieHeader) {
  const sid = parseCookies(cookieHeader).sid; // your cookie name
  if (!sid) return null;

  const session = await yourSessionStore.get(sid);
  if (!session) return null;

  return { userId: session.userId, name: session.name };
}

// May this person open this document at all?
async function canOpenDoc(userId, docId) {
  return await db.userCanSeeDocument(userId, docId);
}

// What may they do in it? The server's answer is the one that counts.
async function roleFor(userId, docId) {
  const p = await db.permissionFor(userId, docId);
  return p.canEdit ? "editor" : p.canSuggest ? "suggester" : "viewer";
}
```

#### Remove the demo shortcuts

Search all three files for TEMPORARY. Each shortcut sits above the correct code, so the correct code takes over once the shortcut is gone.

- index.js has the `DEV_OPEN_ACCESS` block and the `dev-user` return in `getSession`, the `return true` in `canOpenDoc`, and the `return "editor"` in `roleFor`.
- .env has the `DEV_OPEN_ACCESS` line.
- public/index.html has the random guest user, the shared `docId`, the starter text and the license key.

After the shortcuts are gone, every request returns `401` until your session logic is in place. That is the server working as designed.

#### Render the page for the signed-in user

Render the editor page after your session check so the real id, name and role reach `collabConfig`. This route uses Express with your template engine of choice.

```
app.get("/doc/:docId", async (req, res) => {
  const { docId } = req.params;
  const session = await getSession(req.headers.cookie);
  if (!session) return res.redirect("/login");
  if (!(await canOpenDoc(session.userId, docId))) return res.sendStatus(403);

  res.render("document", {
    docId,
    user: {
      id: session.userId,
      name: session.name,
      role: await roleFor(session.userId, docId),
    },
    froalaKey: process.env.FROALA_KEY,
  });
});
```

### Good to know

- Same origin. The collaboration plugin sends REST requests with the browser's default `fetch` settings, which include cookies for same-origin requests. Serve the editor page from the same origin as the API, as this example does with `express.static`, so cookie-based sign-in works.
- Starting content. The relay keeps live edits in memory and holds no history. When someone opens an empty room, the page loads the last copy from `/content`. When others are already editing, the live room takes over.
- Images. The allow list accepts images with `http` or `https` sources, so set up [image upload](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/concepts/image/upload/) to store pasted images as files. Froala adds alignment classes such as `fr-dib` to images. Add `class` to the `img` attributes in `POLICY` to keep that layout in saved content.

### Learn More

- [Collaborative Plugin](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/plugins/collaborative-plugin/)
- [Collaborative Backend API](https://froala.com/wysiwyg-editor/docs/plugins/collaborative-plugin/collaborative-backend-api-reference/)
- [Building a Production-Ready Collaboration Backend for Froala](https://froala.com/blog/editor/tutorials/build-froala-collaboration-backend/)
- [Collaborative Review Workflows in Froala Without a WebSocket Server](https://froala.com/blog/editor/tutorials/froala-async-collaboration-without-websocket/)

